1. Technical Field

The present invention relates to hydraulic telemetry, computational fluid dynamics, cryptographic systems, and real‑time infrastructure integrity. More specifically, the invention concerns a system and method for generating tamper‑proof hydraulic state records by coupling deterministic fluid‑dynamic computation with authenticated encryption and entropy‑driven key generation.

2. Background of the Invention

Hydraulic infrastructure such as pipelines, industrial fluid networks, and water distribution systems require continuous monitoring of pressure, flow rate, turbulence, and other physical parameters. Conventional telemetry systems transmit these measurements through unsecured or partially secured channels, making them vulnerable to tampering, replay attacks, and falsified data injection.

Existing computational fluid dynamics (CFD) systems provide numerical simulations of hydraulic behavior but lack cryptographic guarantees of integrity. Conversely, modern cryptographic systems provide strong data protection but do not incorporate physical invariants or real‑time fluid‑dynamic behavior into their keying or verification mechanisms.

There is therefore a need for a system that integrates physical computation, numerical representation, entropy‑driven cryptography, and tamper‑proof verification into a unified architecture.

3. Summary of the Invention

The invention provides a system and method for generating immutable hydraulic telemetry by combining:

deterministic fluid‑dynamic solvers,

tensor‑based state representation,

kinetic‑entropy computation,

cryptographic key derivation,

authenticated encryption, and

physical and cryptographic verification protocols.

The invention produces encrypted state vectors that are physically grounded, cryptographically sealed, and tamper‑evident. A strict mass‑conservation invariant ensures numerical correctness, while cryptographic integrity hashes ensure data authenticity.

The invention creates a cryptophysical ledger, enabling forensic reconstruction and real‑time infrastructure integrity.

